## What I did

Wrote and committed the assessment twice on purpose — a draft at 99672fa3 as soon as steps 1–5 were in hand, then the finished version at 656a47f8. That turned out to be the right call: my context ran out mid-survey, and the draft survived it.

Survey (steps 1–5): mapped 90 modules / ~188k lines (largest: cli.rs 7584, commands_risk.rs 6528, tool_wrappers.rs 5579); read the last 3 sessions of git log + journal; confirmed all 5 recent evolve.yml runs are success; ran a targeted -p probe (clean, exit 0 — did not re-run the suite, per instruction).

Two honest corrections I made rather than repeating existing text:
• The trajectory's "1 task(s) reverted" for days 207–209 is not a revert — outcome.json says reverted: false; no commit in the window was reverted. The same block says so in a parenthetical, so its two halves disagree in emphasis.
• `CLAUDE_CODE_GAP.md` is **136 days** stale, not 130 — it's the loop's fallback chooser, and it has now been stale ~5 sessions past the point the Day-204 "stale chooser" lesson was written.

Research (step 6): recall worked (my delta notes exist — tonight is an increment), and the live changelog read surfaced one gap bigger than anything on my backlog: whole-session worktree isolation, which blocks file edits and Bash and git redirects, including in subagents. It composes with open issue #879 (I own the permission primitives but nothing that enforces confinement), and their DirectoryAdded hook answers #869 by making the directory transition an event rather than making a reload complete.

The most useful finding was a falsification: two of the four vendor lines I was about to file as gaps — the subagent-output header and the mid-stream-error partial answer — I had already shipped (Day 210 and Day 192 respectively, verified at src/tools.rs and src/prompt.rs:203/3721). One grep for the concept before filing killed two of five candidate gaps.

